it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
any later version.

PampelMuse is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
GNU General Public License for more details.

You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
along with PampelMuse. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.


Provides
--------
The main script of PampelMuse, the <P>otsdam <A>dvanced <M>ulti <P>SF
<E>xtraction A<l>gorithm for <MUSE>. All analysis starts here.

Purpose
-------
The routine first checks if all dependencies for running PampelMuse are
fulfilled. Especially, it checks whether PyQt4 is available on the system.
Afterwards, the subroutines of PampelMuse are loaded; depending on whether
PyQt4 was found on the system, the routines that require graphical output
are loaded or not.

In the next step, the configuration of PampelMuse is loaded, and updated
if requested by the user. The default configuration of PampelMuse is stored
in an xml-file that is provided together with the code. For the subsequent
analysis, the configuration will be written to a namespace that is passed
to the individual subroutines called at the end of initialization. The user
has two options to modify the default configuration from the xml-file before
passing it to the subroutines:

(i) via the command line:
PampelMuse can be called with either of the two options '--fromline' and
'--fromfile'. In the former case, the parameters to be updated should be
provided as comma-separated <key>=<value> pairs in the command line after
'--fromline'. In the latter case, '--fromfile' should be followed by the
name of a plain ascii file that contains the <key>=<value> pairs, one per
row. An overview of the parameters that PampelMuse uses can be found in the
manual.

(ii) via the header of the primary HDU of the PampelMuse PRM-file
If a PRM file exists, the routine will check the header for entries HIERARCH
PAMPELMUSE <KEY> and use the provided values to update the configuration.
Note that values given in the header of the PRM-file have priority over those
provided on the command line.

As a last step before calling the subroutine, the routine opens and verifies
the FITS files containing the IFS data. Note that the routine does NOT load
the data into memory yet but just checks whether the file(s) are valid.

Latest SVN revision
-------------------
397, 2017/02/24
"""
import argparse
import importlib
import logging
import os
import pkgutil
import re
import sys

import numpy as np
from astropy.io import fits

import pampelmuse.routines
from pampelmuse.config import Namespace, PampelMuseConfig
from pampelmuse.psf import Variables
from pampelmuse.routines import initfit, ptsort, singlesrc
from pampelmuse.utils.fits import open_ifs_data

__author__ = "Sebastian Kamann (skamann@astro.physik.uni-goettingen.de)"
__version__ = "0.92.1.0"
__revision__ = 397


logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)


# Two small classes are defined that read the configuration provided by the
# user either from the command-line or from a plain ascii-file and add it as
# a dictionary to the namespace that is created by argparse (NOTE that this
# is not the PampelMuse namespace that is defined below)
# (i) class to read configuration from command line, provided as parameter
#     'action' to the argparse argument '--fromline'
class GetValuesFromLine(argparse.Action):
    """
    Short routine to write 'name=value'-pairs appended to the '--fromline'
    option into a dictionary.
    """
    def __call__(self, parser, namespace, values, option_string=None):
        outdict = {}
        for _val in values[0].split(','):
            val = re.sub(r'\s', '', _val)  # remove whitespace characters
            if len(val.split("=")) != 2:
                raise IOError("Bad parameter definition in line: {0}".format(val))
            name, value = val.split("=")
            outdict[name.upper()] = value
        setattr(namespace, self.dest, outdict)


# (ii) class to read configuration from an ascii file, provided as parameter
#      'action' to the argparse argument '--fromfile'
class GetValuesFromFile(argparse.Action):
    """
    Short routine to extract 'name=value'-pairs from an ASCII-file provided
    using the '--fromfile' option and add them to a dictionary
    """
    def __call__(self, parser, namespace, values, option_string=None):
        outdict = {}
        for _line in values:
            if not len(_line) == 1:  # empty lines have len=1
                if not _line[0] == "#":
                    line = re.sub(r'\s', '', _line)  # remove whitespace characters
                    if not len(line.split("=")) == 2:
                        raise IOError("Invalid line in file '{0}': '{1}'".format(values.name, line))
                    name, value = line.split("=")
                    outdict[name.upper()] = value
        setattr(namespace, self.dest, outdict)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.stdout.write("""
        ,;:.    PampelMuse, version {0:17}    ,;:.
       (::;;)   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   (::;;)
        `;:'                                             `;:'

    (c) 2013-2016 {1}
    """.format(__version__, __author__))

    sys.stdout.write("""
    PampelMuse is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
    it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
    the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
    any later version.

    PampelMuse is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
    but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
    MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
    GNU General Public License for more details.

    You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
    along with PampelMuse. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.

""")
    sys.stdout.flush()

    # Initialize main argument parser for PampelMuse
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="PampelMuse", description="""
    PampelMuse is a routine to extract stellar spectra from IFS data vi PSF
    fitting techniques. It is designed for the analysis of IFS observations
    of crowded stellar fields. As the name suggests, it was originally intended
    to serve as an analysis tool for the Muse spectrograph. Yet, PampelMuse is
    written in a more generic way and should be able to analyze data coming from
    other spectrographs as well. A limitation of the current version is that the
    spaxels of the IFS should be square. The analysis of the data is performed in
    different stages that are carried out by the  various sub-routines provided
    below.""")
    parser.add_argument("prefix", type=str, help="""
    The prefix common to all FITS-files required by PampelMuse. The naming of the
    files should be according to one of the following schemes:\n

    (1) Data, uncertainties, and spectrumMask are included in a single FITS file. In that
    case, a file named PREFIX.fits should be available, containing at least an
    HDU named DATA and one named either ERROR (uncertainties) or STAT (variances).
    The spectrumMask may be included in an additional HDU named MASK.

    (2) Different FITS files are used for data, uncertainties and spectrumMask. In that
    case, the following files should be present:
    (i)   PREFIX.dat.fits - the IFS data
    (ii)  PREFIX.err.fits - the uncertainties of the IFS data
    (iii) PREFIX.msk.fits - optional! - a bad-pixel spectrumMask

    All routines except for INITFIT also require the PampelMuse PRM file, named
    PREFIX.prm.fits. This file is created by the routine INITFIT.""")

    # Note: 'fromfile' and 'fromline' share the same dest 'parameter'. However,
    #       since they are in a 'mutually exclusive group' and their 'default'
    #       is set to argparse.SUPPRESS, at most one of them will contribute to
    #       the namespace.
    group = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=False)
    group.add_argument("--fromfile", type=argparse.FileType('r'), action=GetValuesFromFile, dest="parameter",
                       default=argparse.SUPPRESS, help="""
    Update configuration using the parameters provided in an ASCII-file: One
    parameter per line, name and value separated by '=', lines starting with '#'
    are ignored""")
    group.add_argument("--fromline", type=str, nargs=1, action=GetValuesFromLine, dest="parameter",
                       default=argparse.SUPPRESS, help="""
    Update configuration using the parameters provided in the command line,
    following '--fromline'-string. Again, name and value must be separated
    by '=' (no whitespace!). Individual parameters  must be comma-separated.""")

    subparsers = parser.add_subparsers(help='Available routines')

    # list modules in the pampelmuse.routines package
    pkgpath = os.path.dirname(pampelmuse.routines.__file__)
    modules = [name for _, name, _ in pkgutil.iter_modules([pkgpath])]

    # loop over modules in pampelmuse.routines to find available subroutines
    for m in modules:
        # import module
        if m == 'addwcs':
            continue
        mod = importlib.import_module('pampelmuse.routines.' + m)

        # add subparser
        try:
            mod.add_parser(subparsers)
        except AttributeError:
            continue

    # parse arguments into preliminary namespace
    args = parser.parse_args()

    # A Namespace is created to store the configuration of PampelMuse. The parameters
    # included in the namespace will be added as hierarch keywords to the header of
    # the primary HDU of the PampelMuse PRM file. As an example, the Namespace parameter
    # pampelmuse.initfit.version corresponds to HIERARCH PAMPELMUSE INITFIT VERSION
    pampelmuse = PampelMuseConfig()
    pampelmuse.version = __version__
    pampelmuse.revision = __revision__

    # add name of PRM file to configuration
    pampelmuse.prefix = args.prefix
    pampelmuse.filename = "{0}.prm.fits".format(args.prefix)

    # initialize (sub)namespace for subroutine that will be called and add command-line arguments to this subspace.
    ns = Namespace()
    for key, value in args.__dict__.iteritems():
        if key not in ["func", "parameter", "prefix"]:
            setattr(ns, key, value)
    setattr(pampelmuse, args.func.__name__, ns)

    # initialize the configuration of PampelMuse
    # i) read default configuration that is saved in an XML-file that is shipped with PampelMuse
    # To check the default configuration, open the XML file using any web browser, e.g.
    # >>> firefox $pampelmuse_path/pampelmuse/config/data/PMparams.xml

    # ii) if prm file exists, update the configuration using the primary header hierarch keys. Note that these keywords
    # can be either directly connected to PampelMuse (e.g. HIERARCH PAMPELMUSE IFU) or to one of its subroutines
    # (e.g. HIERARCH PAMPELMUSE INITFIT VERSION). In the latter case, the key will be added to the namespace of the
    # corresponding subroutine.
    if args.func not in [initfit, singlesrc, ptsort]:
        pampelmuse.readHeader(fits.getheader(pampelmuse.filename))

    # iii) update configuration using command line input. Note that during the analysis, i.e. when calling a routine
    # different from INITFIT or SINGLESRC, only a subset of the parameters can be changed. For example, it makes no
    # sense to change the RESLIM parameter after selecting the sources.
    if hasattr(args, "parameter"):
        only_changeable = (args.func not in [initfit, singlesrc])  # check if only subset of parameters can be updated
        for key, value in args.parameter.iteritems():
            success = pampelmuse.update_parameter(key, value, only_changeable=only_changeable)
            if success == 2:
                logger.error("Invalid entry in user configuration: '{0}={1}".format(key, value))
                raise IOError("Error encountered while updating configuration.")

    # iv) The PSF is handled specially - to facilitate its usage afterwards, a dedicated namespace is created in which
    # all the information is stored
    if args.func in [initfit, singlesrc]:
        pampelmuse.psf = Namespace()
    
        # check if requested profile exists in pampelmuse.core.psf.variables.Variables
        try:
            parnames = Variables.PROFILES[pampelmuse.psftype]
        except KeyError:
            logger.critical("Unknown PSF type: '{0}'".format(pampelmuse.psftype))
        pampelmuse.psf.profile = pampelmuse.psftype

        # translate parameter PSFFIT from PampelMuse configuration into boolean array indicating which variables of the
        # PSF are fitted.
        freepar = np.zeros(len(parnames), dtype=np.bool)
        for i in xrange(freepar.size, 0, -1):
            if pampelmuse.psffit >= 2**i:
                freepar[i-1] = True
                pampelmuse.psffit -= 2**i

        # create dedicated (sub)namespace for the PSF containing three attributes per variable:
        # parname  - the name of the variable
        # parvalue - the (initial) value of the variable
        # freepar  - whether or not the variable is a free fit parameter
        for name in parnames:
            i = parnames.index(name)
            setattr(pampelmuse.psf, "parname{0}".format(i), name)
            setattr(pampelmuse.psf, "parvalue{0}".format(i), pampelmuse.psfpars[i])
            setattr(pampelmuse.psf, "freepar{0}".format(i), bool(freepar[i]))

    if args.func == initfit and pampelmuse.initfit.sourcelist is None:
        pampelmuse.initfit.sourcelist = pampelmuse.catalog

    delattr(pampelmuse, "psffit")
    delattr(pampelmuse, "psfpars")

    # set up logging level and format; therefore complete logger configuration needs to be reset
    # see http://stackoverflow.com/questions/12158048/changing-loggings-basicconfig-which-is-already-set
    LOGLEVELS = {0: logging.CRITICAL, 1: logging.ERROR, 2: logging.WARNING, 3: logging.INFO, 4: logging.DEBUG}
    for handler in logging.root.handlers:
        logging.root.removeHandler(handler)
    logging.basicConfig(level=LOGLEVELS[pampelmuse.verbose],
                        format="%(asctime)s[%(levelname)-8s]: %(message)s",  # - %(pathname)s[%(lineno)s]",
                        datefmt='%m/%d/%Y %H:%M:%S')

    # check whether dependencies for graphical backends are fulfilled
    graphics = True
    try:
        from PyQt4 import QtCore, QtGui
    except ImportError:
        logger.error("Cannot load 'PyQt4'-modules")
        QtCore = None
        QtGui = None
        graphics = False
    try:
        import matplotlib
    except ImportError:
        logger.error("Cannot load 'matplotlib'-modules")
        matplotlib = None
        graphics = False
    if not graphics:
        if pampelmuse.interact:
            logger.error("Cannot use PampelMuse GUI. Interaction mode set to False.")
            pampelmuse.interact = False
        else:
            logger.warning("Cannot use PampelMuse GUI.")

    # Used as a test: an additional logger that gives the file and line where a warning message was triggered
    # if pampelmuse.verbose==4:
    #     ch = logging.StreamHandler()
    #     ch.setLevel(logging.WARNING)
    #     ch.setFormatter(logging.Formatter("%(pathname)s [%(lineno)-4s]: %(message)s"))
    #     logging.getLogger().addHandler(ch)

    if args.func != ptsort:
        # open IFS data
        pampelmuse.ifs_data = open_ifs_data(args.prefix)

        # set range of layers to be analyzed properly (in case negative values were provided)
        # access start/stop information - get positive layer numbers for first and last layer that should be analysed
        if pampelmuse.start < 0 and pampelmuse.ifs_data.nlayer is not None:
            pampelmuse.start = pampelmuse.ifs_data.nlayer + pampelmuse.start
        if pampelmuse.stop < 0 and pampelmuse.ifs_data.nlayer is not None:
            pampelmuse.stop = pampelmuse.ifs_data.nlayer + pampelmuse.stop
        logger.info("Layer range used in analysis: {0}--{1}".format(pampelmuse.start, pampelmuse.stop))

    # execute the function connected to the requested (sub)routine
    args.func(pampelmuse)
